<div style="background-color: #f0f8ff; border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain future career or educational plans. Interactive: Participate in role-plays about job interviews.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain to peers the required skills for a future job (e.g., "You need to be good at math to work in engineering."). Can participate in a role-play to practice for a job interview, asking and answering detailed questions about strengths, goals, and required skills (e.g., "What is your greatest strength?" "I am a quick learner, and I enjoy working with technology."). Can share future educational plans and reasons (e.g., "I will study engineering to work on technology projects.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e0eaf4;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various images of "future jobs" or "in-demand industries" (e.g., AI specialist, remote worker, green energy engineer, digital content creator). Students work in pairs to discuss: "What jobs do you see in these pictures? What skills do you think people will need for these jobs in the future?" The teacher encourages the use of vocabulary such as 'technology,' 'adaptability,' 'innovation,' 'demand,' and verbs like 'require,' 'evolve,' 'acquire.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using future simple and modals, for example: "An AI specialist will definitely need strong analytical skills." "Many future jobs may require people to be adaptable and innovative." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "automation," "advancement," "adaptability," "employment," and "artificial intelligence," emphasizing stress patterns. The teacher also demonstrates how to pronounce /dʒ/ in "change" and /ʒ/ in "vision" when discussing evolving job roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the concept of 'future skills' and 'in-demand jobs'.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a job is 'in-demand,' does it mean many people want it or many companies need workers for it?" (Expected answer: Many companies need workers.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'you will acquire new skills,' does that mean you already have them or you need to learn them?" (Expected answer: You need to learn them.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording or short dialogue script (e.g., 2-3 minutes) between a career counselor and a 10th-grade student discussing future career paths and the skills required. The dialogue will include examples of future simple for predictions, future continuous for long-term goals, and modals for possibilities, as well as target vocabulary like 'career,' 'education,' 'technology,' 'advancement,' 'adaptability,' 'innovation,' 'remote work,' 'employment,' 'opportunities,' 'industries.' The register is semi-formal but encouraging.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing personal career goals and plans, and asking about future career paths.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue once for gist: "What is the main topic of the conversation?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen a second time and answer True/False questions based on the dialogue (e.g., "The student will study medicine." "The counselor suggests developing technological skills.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete sentences based on the dialogue (e.g., "In five years, the student will be ______ (verb in future continuous) in a tech company." "The counselor says new jobs ______ (modal) emerge due to automation.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing future simple for predictions, future continuous for long-term goals, and modals for possibilities, along with target vocabulary (jobs, skills, future, demand, advancement, technology, innovation, adaptability, education, industries, employment).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Gap-fill & Pronunciation):</b> Students complete sentences orally or in writing using 'will,' 'will be + -ing,' or 'may/might' + verb. Example: "Many jobs in the next decade ______ (require) digital literacy." "By 2030, most professionals ______ (work) with AI tools." "New industries ______ (emerge) constantly." Teacher monitors, corrects grammar and pronunciation, specifically focusing on the stress in words like 'technology,' 'innovation,' and 'advancement.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Transformation & Opinion Sharing):</b> Teacher provides sentence stems about future careers, and students complete them, then share with a partner. Example stems: "I believe that in 10 years, people will be working more in ______." "Future jobs may require skills like ______ and ______." "My educational plans will involve ______ because I want to ______." Partners listen and give brief feedback, practicing active listening and asking follow-up questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Q&A):</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A asks questions about Partner B's dream job and required skills. Partner B answers, using the target grammar and vocabulary. Then they switch roles.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What career do you foresee for yourself in the future?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific skills do you think you will need to acquire for that job?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In five years, what will you be doing to advance your career?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you think automation may replace some tasks in your chosen field?"</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher encourages using contrastive sounds like /dʒ/ in "challenge" and /ʒ/ in "vision" where applicable in their responses.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play: "Future Job Interview Simulation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student acts as a job interviewer, and the other acts as a candidate applying for a "dream job" in a future industry (e.g., an 'AI ethics consultant,' 'virtual reality architect,' 'remote work coordinator,' 'sustainable development specialist').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The interviewer asks about the candidate's future career goals, educational plans, and relevant skills for the chosen job.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The candidate must explain their future educational plans (e.g., "I will study engineering to work on technology projects."), describe their strengths and skills (e.g., "I am a quick learner, and I enjoy working with technology. I believe adaptability is a key skill for this role."), and explain how they will contribute to the company's future (e.g., "I will be developing innovative solutions..."). They must use future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibilities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to incorporate as much target vocabulary (e.g., 'innovation,' 'demand,' 'advancement,' 'adaptability,' 'remote work,' 'employment,' 'opportunities,' 'industries') and practice pronunciation (stress in multisyllabic words, contrastive sounds) as possible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should also practice adapting their tone and formality (sociolinguistic competence) based on the "interview" context, contrasting it with peer discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each role-play should last about 3-4 minutes per pair. The teacher circulates, offering support and noting examples of good language use.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Future Job Interview Simulation" role-play in front of the class or in small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback Form):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, correct stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., 'adaptability,' 'innovation'), and distinction of contrastive sounds (e.g., /dʒ/ vs. /ʒ/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporation of at least 5-7 words from the lesson's vocabulary list (e.g., 'future,' 'demand,' 'technology,' 'adaptability,' 'innovation,' 'employment,' 'opportunities').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Consistent and accurate use of future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Coherence:</b> Explaining future career/educational plans clearly and logically.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Interaction:</b> Effective participation in the role-play (asking/answering questions appropriately).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic/Sociolinguistic Awareness:</b> Adapting tone and formality appropriate for a job interview.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new skill I learned about for a future career is ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I still need more practice explaining ______ about my future plans."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about discussing future job skills and my career plans in English?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary or grammar from today's lesson will I try to use more in future conversations about jobs?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the speaking activities worked well to promote fluency and accuracy?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to effectively use future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibilities? What specific challenges did they face?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., sentence starters, more explicit pronunciation drills) might be needed for students who struggled with expressing their future plans or using the target vocabulary?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I ensure the continued application of sociolinguistic awareness in future speaking tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will apply the vocabulary, grammar, and ideas discussed today to write a formal career statement or a short essay about their future educational and career plans, incorporating the skills required for evolving job markets.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>